<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch2-page4-order-dependence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Iterated Elimination of Dominated Strategies</title>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-8">
    <title>Section 2.8, Iterated Elimination of Dominated Strategies</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.8.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's the payoff matrix from Section 2.9, the Battle of the Bismarck
    Sea. We'll read the historical context later. For now, let's
    just look at the iterated elimination itself.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bismarck-sea">
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Imamura sail north</cell>
        <cell>Imamura sail south</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Kenney search north</cell>
        <cell>(1&#8531;, &#8722;1&#8531;)</cell>
        <cell>(1&#189;, &#8722;1&#189;)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Kenney search south</cell>
        <cell>(1, &#8722;1)</cell>
        <cell>(2, &#8722;2)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Before any elimination, neither of Kenney's strategies dominates the
    other. Let's do the iteration, starting with Imamura, in the
    interactive below.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bismarck-sea-elimination">
    <caption>Iterated elimination of dominated strategies, Battle of the Bismarck Sea.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-bismarck-sea-elimination"
      iframe="ch2-graph/bismarck-sea-elimination.html" width="100%" aspect="4:3">
      <static>
        <p>
          The payoff matrix from Section 2.9, with buttons asking which
          strategy dominates for Imamura, then for Kenney, crossing out
          eliminated strategies as you go.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-9">
    <title>Section 2.9, The Battle of the Bismarck Sea</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.9.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-10">
    <title>Section 2.10, Normal Form of a Game in Extensive Form with Complete Information</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.10.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Section 2.10 converts Big Monkey and Little Monkey, from Chapter 1,
    into normal form. Explore the connection between the two
    representations below.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-normal-extensive-bmlm">
    <caption>Big Monkey and Little Monkey, normal form and extensive form side by side.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-normal-extensive-bmlm"
      iframe="ch2-graph/normal-extensive-bmlm.html" width="100%" aspect="16:9">
      <static>
        <p>
          The normal form payoff matrix on the left and the game tree on
          the right. Clicking a matrix entry highlights the matching path
          through the tree.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Now let's use iterated elimination on this normal form matrix.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bmlm-elimination-1">
    <caption>Iterated elimination of weakly dominated strategies, Big Monkey and Little Monkey (first ordering).</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-bmlm-elimination-1"
      iframe="ch2-graph/bmlm-normal-form-elimination.html" width="100%" aspect="4:3">
      <static>
        <p>
          Step-by-step iterated elimination on the Big Monkey and Little
          Monkey matrix, ending at the dominant strategy equilibrium
          (w, cw).
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Let's try a different order of elimination on the same matrix.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-bmlm-elimination-2">
    <caption>Iterated elimination of weakly dominated strategies, Big Monkey and Little Monkey (second ordering).</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-bmlm-elimination-2"
      iframe="ch2-graph/bmlm-normal-form-elimination-order2.html" width="100%" aspect="4:3">
      <static>
        <p>
          The same matrix, eliminated in a different valid order, ending
          with two surviving strategy profiles instead of one.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-2-11">
    <title>Section 2.11, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 2</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 2.11.</alert>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Same matrix, two valid elimination orders, two different results. The
    first ordering pinned down a unique dominant strategy equilibrium; the
    second only pinned down Big Monkey's strategy, leaving Little Monkey's
    undetermined. This is the order-dependence Section 2.8 warned about:
    iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies always gives the
    same answer no matter the order, but iterated elimination of weakly
    dominated strategies does not.
  </p>

</section>
